// Stochastic %K reclaim. %K measures where the close sits inside its own
// fourteen-bar range, 0..100. This rule fires on the bar %K crosses back up
// through 20 - not while it is under 20 - which is the whole difference
// between an edge and a level, and it needs `k[1]` to express it.
// REGIME: a range, or a pullback inside an uptrend. Do NOT fire in a sustained
// downtrend: %K pins near zero there and every small bounce produces a
// reclaim, so the rule turns into a metronome that buys each leg down.
// INPUTS: high, low, close (series<float>). No other host signal.
// CONDITIONS: %K at or above 20 this bar and below 20 last bar. One bar, one
// signal, then silence until it goes back under.
// INVALIDATION: %K falling back under 20 within a bar or two, or a new low in
// price while %K makes a higher low - the second is the classic setup that
// looks like this one and is not.
// SHAPE: 1h; a flush to the bottom of the recent range that turns.
// NOT stochastic_oversold: that is the level test on baseline IR - it fires on
// every bar %K spends under 20, which in a downtrend is most of them. This
// fires once per excursion. If you are comparing fire counts between the two,
// the ratio is the point, not a bug.
// CALIBRATED ON: crypto, 1h. %K is bounded 0..100, so 20 travels across
// instruments in a way an absolute price threshold does not; what does not
// travel is how long an instrument sits pinned at the bottom of its range,
// which is what decides whether "reclaim" means anything.
strategy StochasticReclaim {

    param window: int = 14
    param oversold: float = 20.0

    input high: series<float>
    input low: series<float>
    input close: series<float>

    let k = STOCH_K(high, low, close, window)

    every 1h {

        if k >= oversold and k[1] < oversold {

            buy(BTCUSD, 0.6)

        }

    }

}
